response:
{
  "scenarios": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "title": "The Early Morning Fog",
      "difficulty": 1,
      "narrative": "You've just woken up from a full 8 hours of sleep — your best rest yet. But instead of feeling refreshed, your mind feels sluggish, wrapped in a strange fog. The sunlight streaming through your window feels almost irritating. Your phone buzzes with messages from friends asking to hang out later. Part of you wants to cancel and stay in bed, but another part knows that might just make the fog worse. You have 30 minutes before you need to leave the house.",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Force yourself up immediately: splash cold water on your face, step outside for 5 minutes of fresh air, and send a quick 'See you later' message to your friends.",
          "strategy": "Behavioral Activation"
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "It's okay to rest. You cancel on your friends, rationalizing that a tired brain needs recovery. You'll get together another day.",
          "strategy": "Avoidance/Withdrawal"
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Sit with the fog for 2 minutes without judgment. Take 5 slow breaths. Then check: what does your body actually need — movement, nutrition, social contact? Choose one small action that feels sustainable right now.",
          "strategy": "Emotional Regulation/Mindful Awareness"
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Analyze why you feel this way: start mentally listing possible causes (medication side effects, dehydration, lack of dream recall) until you've identified a likely culprit — then address it systematically.",
          "strategy": "Analytical Problem-Solving"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "title": "The Overlooked Variable",
      "difficulty": 3,
      "narrative": "You're working on a project proposal that's due in two hours. Halfway through, you realize you misread a key data entry point in the team's shared document. If you correct it now, you'll need to rework most of your section. Your team lead noticed the error earlier but didn't mention it, assuming you'd catch it yourself. The rest of the team is unaware.",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Announce the error publicly in the team chat immediately, show transparent ownership, delegate parts of the rework to teammates, and ask the lead for a 30-minute extension to make the fix together.",
          "strategy": "Accountability/Transparency"
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Correct it quietly on your own. You can handle it — no need to embarrass yourself in front of the team. The lead probably expected this anyway.",
          "strategy": "Self-Reliant Suppression"
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Pause. Take a breath. Acknowledge the frustration without letting it drive a rushed fix. Then determine: is this a calculation error, or a process flaw? Communicate the gap to just the lead first, proposing a corrected timeline.",
          "strategy": "Emotional Regulation + Planned Communication"
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Send a quick message saying you discovered the issue and need to verify something but don't correct it until after the meeting. Flag it as a minor note for the next revision cycle.",
          "strategy": "Minimization/Delay"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "title": "The Uninvited Memory",
      "difficulty": 4,
      "narrative": "On your afternoon commute, you pass a park where you once had a very painful conversation with an old friend during one of your hardest periods. Unbidden, the memory surfaces — fragments of words, the cold bench, the feeling of being alone even while surrounded by people. The feeling sits heavy in your chest. You walk faster, trying to physically leave it behind.",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Keep walking and distract yourself immediately: put on music, call someone, scroll your phone. Don't let the past steal today from you.",
          "strategy": "Suppression/Distraction"
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Sit on a nearby bench and let the memory surface fully. You don't know why it hurt then, but you owe it to yourself to process it now — maybe journal for the first time in months, or contact a counselor.",
          "strategy": "Courageous Exposure"
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Acknowledge the feeling — 'That was a painful moment, and I survived it' — then gently redirect your attention to one grounding sensory detail around you: the color of the sky, the sound of children playing.",
          "strategy": "Mindful Redirection"
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Create a new memory here. Call a trusted person and arrange to meet here for coffee on purpose — take ownership of the space and rewrite its emotional meaning.",
          "strategy": "Active Reclamation"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "title": "The Medication Question",
      "difficulty": 5,
      "narrative": "During a casual group dinner, someone casually mentions that they've been on anxiety medication for years and recently tapered off. They rave about how much 'clearer' they feel without it. Your friends turn to you, knowing you're also on meds, curious about your take. You feel a surprising pang of doubt — have you considered quitting yours? Your doctor has been encouraging, and your sleep has improved, but the seed of doubt is planted.",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Defend your choice firmly: 'I'm on them because they work for me — end of discussion.' Change the topic quickly.",
          "strategy": "Defensive Closure"
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Poll your friends privately afterward. 'Anyone else considered going off meds? What made you stick with it or not?' Base your decision on the group's consensus.",
          "strategy": "Social Comparison"
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Acknowledge their experience, share that you've questioned your own medication at times, and reflect honestly: 'For now, the benefits outweigh the concerns — but I appreciate the conversation.' Then privately plan to discuss with your doctor.",
          "strategy": "Integrated Self-Reflection"
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Research tonight: read half a dozen peer-reviewed articles about discontinuation — taper protocols, rebound effects, neurological adaptation — before forming any opinion.",
          "strategy": "Information-Seeking"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "title": "The Midnight Boundary",
      "difficulty": 7,
      "narrative": "It's 10:45 PM. You've completed your sleep hygiene routine: screens off, tea by the bed, a book instead of your phone. Then an emergency call comes from your office — your inbox has gone down right before a client demo tomorrow at 8 AM. Your colleague is panicking. 'Is there any chance you can log in and fix this? You're the only one who knows the admin panel.' The book on your lap already smells like calm.",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Put the phone on 'Do Not Disturb.' You've earned your rest. If the demo fails, it's a systems failure — not your personal responsibility. Reduce tomorrow's anxiety by protecting tonight's sleep.",
          "strategy": "Boundary-Setting/Prioritization"
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Warm tea forgotten, you log in immediately and spend the night troubleshooting. You tell yourself it'll be quick — but three hours later, you're still staring at the screen.",
          "strategy": "Compulsive Caretaking"
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "You take the call calmly. Listen to your colleague's panic without absorbing it. Walk them through the fix step-by-step over the phone while staying in bed — laptop closed, voice only. If it's not resolved in 15 minutes, agree on a backup plan (rolled-back upgrade, manual demo with Screenshots, or asking another teammate).",
          "strategy": "Calm Moderation + Depersonalization"
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Refuse outright: 'I'm signed off until 8 AM.' You don't explain or apologize — a hard boundary with no room for negotiation.",
          "strategy": "Rigid Boundary"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "title": "The Echo of Inadequacy",
      "difficulty": 4,
      "narrative": "You're in a workplace meeting where someone casually critiques your proposal. The comment feels off — not about the content, but about you, as though your thinking isn't sharp enough. You notice a familiar heat rising in your chest. The others are looking at you, waiting for a reply. You don't want to overreact, but the old feeling of being 'less than' is knocking louder than ever.",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Fire back with a pointed remark about their recent mistakes. You won't be anyone's punching bag — even subtly.",
          "strategy": "Reactive Retaliation"
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Stuff it down. Nod, say 'Noted,' and move on. You'll journal about it later — maybe.",
          "strategy": "Suppression/Compliance"
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Pause. Notice the heat. Take one breath. Ask for clarification: 'Could you elaborate on what feels unclear to you about the approach?' Respond to the content, not the perceived subtext.",
          "strategy": "Emotional Labeling + Assertive Inquiry"
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Flag it after the meeting: send a private message asking if something personal was meant by their comment. Proactively resolve any misunderstanding before it festers.",
          "strategy": "Post-Hoc Resolution"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": 7,
      "title": "The Gratitude Gap",
      "difficulty": 2,
      "narrative": "Your journaling app sends a notification: a new 'weekly reflection' prompt. 'Name three things you're genuinely grateful for this week.' You stare at the screen. You slept well most nights, you had one laugh with a coworker, you finished a book… but a familiar murmur says, 'These don't count. I'm not doing enough to feel grateful.'",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Force yourself to list three items anyway — even if they feel hollow. 'Discipline creates motivation. Fake it till you make it.'",
          "strategy": "Behavioral Override"
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Close the app. Gratitude journaling isn't for you right now — maybe another time. No use forcing positivity when it feels meaningless.",
          "strategy": "Withdrawal/Disengagement"
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Sit with the resistance for a moment. 'I notice I don't feel grateful — and that's okay. What WOULD feel truthful?' Write one true sentence, even a simple one — 'I'm glad Tuesday's rain made the train ride quieter.' No pressure for more.",
          "strategy": "Acceptance + Gentle Exploration"
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Skip gratitude today. Instead, write down what you WISH had happened this week — the gaps, the desires. Point your emotional energy toward what you want to feel qualified to appreciate.",
          "strategy": "Desire Exploration"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": 8,
      "title": "The Unexpected Ally",
      "difficulty": 6,
      "narrative": "You're at a park listening to a podcast about emotional intelligence when a stranger on the next bench asks what you're learning. Expecting to be annoyed, you give a short summary. They listen intently, share they've been in therapy for months, and ask if you'd ever support a free 'peer listening group' they're starting. Your schedule already feels overly committed, but something about their sincerity moves you.",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Politely decline: 'I barely have time to keep my own head above water. I can't take on someone else's recovery.'",
          "strategy": "Self-Protection/Decline"
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Agree immediately. How can you say no to someone who finally understands? You'll make time — even if it means canceling one of your own support routines.",
          "strategy": "Compulsive Altruism"
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Pause. Acknowledge their courage: 'That's a brave idea. Can I think about it — would you be open to me reaching out next week after checking my current commitments?'",
          "strategy": "Boundaried Openness"
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Say yes — but quietly calculate: what might serving others give YOU that your own recovery doesn't? Frame your acceptance as adding something to your own toolkit first.",
          "strategy": "Strategic Engagement"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": 9,
      "title": "The Plateau",
      "difficulty": 6,
      "narrative": "You've been prioritizing sleep hygiene for 30 days straight: consistent bedtime, no screens, regular exercise, evening journaling. It's working — your sleep tracker shows deep improvements, and your mood is steadier than it has been in months. But yesterday, you woke up exhausted again. Old thoughts creep in: 'See? You'll never really get better.' You stare at the ceiling, feeling the familiar weight of disappointment.",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Accept it philosophically: 'Life isn't linear. Rest when tired, rise when rested. No need to panic.' Then sleep in.",
          "strategy": "Philosophical Detachment"
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Treat the exhaustion as a puzzle to solve. Immediately audit your day: screen use, caffeine timing, stress levels. Find the 'leak'.",
          "strategy": "Analytical Diagnosis"
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Feel the sadness fully — one day of bad sleep doesn't erase 30 days of growth. Notice the thought: 'I'll never get better' — that's your brain protecting you from hope, not a fact. Take the day as it comes.",
          "strategy": "Self-Compassion + Cognitive Defusion"
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Step outside immediately. 15 minutes of morning sunlight, no journal, no tracker. Let your body remember what it felt like before you started 'optimizing' sleep.",
          "strategy": "Embodied Reset"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": 10,
      "title": "The Feedback Loop",
      "difficulty": 8,
      "narrative": "You're three weeks into a new role at work. Your latest project just received praise from leadership — but one teammate privately told you, 'Great presentation. Shame the analysis section felt rushed.' You know this person usually gives solid feedback. Meanwhile, another colleague forwarded you a client email expressing concern about the timeline. Before, this combination of high praise and sharp critique would have sent you spiraling. Today, you notice your chest tightening but you haven't spiraled — yet you feel paralyzed, unable to process either piece of information clearly. The meeting debrief is in 15 minutes.",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Split the difference: acknowledge both the praise and the critique equally. In the debrief, summarize: 'Thank you all — one thing that worked was X, one thing I'd refine is Y.' Hold both truths in the same breath.",
          "strategy": "Integration/And Thinking"
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Prioritize the positive. The client's timeline concern is standard; your teammate's comment is unsolicited. In the meeting, focus on what's being celebrated and let the rest dissolve.",
          "strategy": "Selective Attention"
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Excuse yourself from the debrief. You're not equipped to process this today — you'll request a 1:1 tomorrow when your stress levels are lower and your thinking is clearer.",
          "strategy": "Strategic Withdrawal"
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Go to a private space for 5 minutes. Breathe. Write two columns: 'What was helpful' and 'What was noise.' Once separated, you'll know what to address. Then walk into the debrief with clarity.",
          "strategy": "Cognitive Sorting + Composure"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
